f 17592186124491 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125357 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125358 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125357 #edb/ref 1061 17530 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125357 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "80c4bbb9-f58d-5db9-9875-a6de44dddc60" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125357 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125358 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125359 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125358 #edb/ref 1061 17531 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125358 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "e23604b2-7d9d-5701-83b0-ef4dd1b95b45" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125358 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186125767 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125359 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125501 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125359 #edb/ref 1061 17533 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125359 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f4b1b6b1-2c84-53dc-b2e9-6ae8617800c7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125359 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125360 #edb/ref 1061 17535 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125360 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "a7664106-d8c0-540f-9ff4-1553bdb9d7a4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125360 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186124494 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125501 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186125360 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125501 #edb/ref 1061 27297 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125501 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "49ef4ed6-562f-5c33-91e3-3f02216c2e4f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125501 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186125823 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125766 #edb/ref 1064 "Given two bases $\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}$ and $\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}$ of a vector space $V,$ the **change-of-coordinates matrix from $\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}$ to $\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}$** is the matrix $P_{\\small{\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}}\\to{\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}}},$ whose columns are the coordinates of the vectors from $\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}$ relative to the basis $\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}$.\n\nIn expanded form, the matrix looks as follows:\n![](images/06/062187705e6074afa6b5356be64f8deb878be540e2c3b236b22dbd244b872cc6.svg)\n\nFor example, let's consider two bases $\\mathcal B$ and $\\mathcal C$ of $\\mathbb{R}^2$ given by \n\n$$\n\\mathcal{B}=\\{\\mathbf{b}_1,\\mathbf{b}_2\\}, \\qquad\\mathcal{C}=\\{\\mathbf{c}_1,\\mathbf{c}_2\\}.\n$$\n\nIf we happen to know that\n\n![](images/32/32409a45b5ebcf6b8c1cc43404126b514066ace8d8c71bcec2e99f759833ec84.svg) \n\nthen the change-of-coordinates matrix from $\\mathcal{B}$ to $\\mathcal{C}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{B\\to C}= & [\\begin{aligned}-12 & 3 \\\\ 11 & 6\\end{aligned}]. \\\\  & \\,{↑}_{[{b}_{1}]_{C}}\\,{↑}_{[{b}_{2}]_{C}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe beauty of the change-of-coordinates matrix is that it allows us to express the vector $[\\mathbf x]_{\\mathcal B}$ in the basis $\\mathcal C$ via the transformation\n\n$$\n[\\mathbf x]_{\\mathcal C} = P_{\\small\\mathcal{B}\\to\\mathcal{C}} \\cdot [\\mathbf x]_{\\mathcal B}.\n$$\n\nThis can be visualized as shown below.\n\n![](images/ed/ede6eb17f0667a8974902bf2d181150eeb3c665faa3059bb818bb9a61b631511.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125766 #edb/ref 1065 3213 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125766 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "46d12d06-cf6a-5c5d-bb38-8670b3501be4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125766 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125767 #edb/ref 1064 "Since the columns of a change-of-coordinates matrix are linearly independent, the matrix must be invertible.\n\nIn fact, the change-of-coordinates matrix from $\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}$ to $\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}$ and the change-of-coordinates matrix from $\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}$ to $\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}$ are the inverses of each other:\n\n$$\nP_{\\small{\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}}\\to{\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}}}^{-1} = P_{\\small{\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}}\\to{\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}}}\n\t\\qquad\\textrm{and}\\qquad\n\tP_{\\small{\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}}\\to{\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}}}^{-1} = P_{\\small{\\color{blue}\\mathcal{B}}\\to{\\color{red}\\mathcal{C}}}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125767 #edb/ref 1065 3215 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125767 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "f2fa55fd-270b-5d4e-89e4-ec0fe2c4e0c0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125767 #edb/ref 1067 "The Inverse of a Change-of-Coordinates Matrix" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125823 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose that we have bases $\\mathcal{B}=\\{ \\mathbf{b}_1,\\mathbf{b}_2 \\}$ and $\\mathcal{C}=\\{ \\mathbf{c}_1,\\mathbf{c}_2 \\},$ and we want to find the change-of-coordinates matrix from $\\mathcal{B}$ to $\\mathcal{C},$ denoted $P_{\\small\\mathcal{B}\\to\\mathcal{C}}.$\n\nRecall that the columns of $P_{\\small\\mathcal{B}\\to\\mathcal{C}}$ are the coordinates of the vectors of $\\mathcal{B}$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{C},$ that is\n![](images/fb/fb63e6dcb2c7b0f8417e4f3fd394d42fa4bb84017552779903c40e4114639ac6.svg)\n\nTo compute $P_{\\small{\\color{black}\\mathcal{B}}\\to{\\color{black}\\mathcal{C}}},$ we need to find the coordinates of $\\mathbf{b}_1$ and $\\mathbf{b}_2$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{C}.$ We can do this simultaneously by considering the matrix\n![](images/64/644f78998195b8be26cb21bf5d680fbebed808d8f412769f9a3b06901533e87d.svg)\n\nIf we reduce the left-hand side of $M$ to reduced row-echelon form using Gaussian elimination, $P_{\\small\\mathcal{B}\\to\\mathcal{C}}$ will emerge on the right-hand side:\n\n![](images/52/52f73b32e9700813960a9c544b71fedcce865ed3c452094be36615459663be9d.svg)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125823 #edb/ref 1065 4439 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125823 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "a6e71bf0-0662-5ac2-abc9-4edf7b085968" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186125823 #edb/ref 1067 "Computing the Change-of-Coordinates Matrix Using Row-Reduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244462 #edb/ref 1080 "[\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 \\\\ 9 & 7\\end{aligned}]" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244462 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244462 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "1006640e-9d75-599c-877c-96d359a3fa69" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244891 #edb/ref 1080 "[\\begin{aligned}4 & -9 \\\\ -3 & 7\\end{aligned}]" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244891 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244891 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "42160313-d20d-5459-8d6f-cbc3455b020c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186245135 #edb/ref 1080 "[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 16\\end{aligned}]" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186245135 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186245135 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "5d256c9b-ca10-5ea9-a696-f3ed6a0a6b0e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186245356 #edb/ref 1080 "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 17\\end{aligned}]" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186245356 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186245356 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "74e82f9c-7a8a-5418-962f-6a02b594b33e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186245359 #edb/ref 1080 "[\\begin{aligned}4 & -3 \\\\ -9 & 7\\end{aligned}]" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186245359 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186245359 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "757412d1-09bb-588e-8eab-d5175af0ed23" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186245798 #edb/ref 1080 "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ 4 & \\frac{1}{17}\\end{aligned}]" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186245798 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186245798 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "a5603449-58ca-55fa-b9cf-370a1ffff990" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186246190 #edb/ref 1080 "[\\begin{aligned}7 & -3 \\\\ -9 & 4\\end{aligned}]" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186246190 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186246190 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "d2e37099-402c-5b14-93b8-cb301499b55a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186246323 #edb/ref 1080 "[\\begin{aligned}9 & -7 \\\\ -4 & 3\\end{aligned}]" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186246323 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186246323 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "e1d65427-e485-5f40-ae92-d2540533f8bc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186246380 #edb/ref 1080 "[\\begin{aligned}1 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 4\\end{aligned}]" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186246380 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186246380 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "e9322699-dec2-5ff2-921c-2b01f57dd42a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186246412 #edb/ref 1080 "[\\begin{aligned}1 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 17\\end{aligned}]" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186246412 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186246412 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "eda62fbe-9a7c-5138-b269-2ee24eacf510" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186246829 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186245135 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186246829 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186245356 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186246829 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186245798 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186246829 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186246380 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186246829 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186246412 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186246829 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186246829 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186246829 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186246412 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186246829 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "a077ac69-607f-57ea-a0ce-e946298ab8c4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186246838 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186244462 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186246838 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186244891 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186246838 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186245359 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186246838 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186246190 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186246838 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186246323 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186246838 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186246838 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186246838 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186245359 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186246838 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "a490c9f0-7a30-569b-b5f7-30e0b6190adc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186247349 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246838 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186247349 #edb/ref 1069 "q-113413" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186247349 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186247349 #edb/ref 1071 "The change-of-coordinates matrix from the basis $C$ to the basis $B$ is given by\n${P}_{C\\to B}=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 3 \\\\ 9 & 4\\end{aligned}].$ Find the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C$  ." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186247349 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6524c152-aeba-5139-803b-30e61293f48c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186247349 #edb/ref 1074 "The change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C$ is the inverse of the change-of-coordinates matrix from $C$ to $B.$ Therefore, we simply need to find the inverse of the given matrix.\n\nComputing the inverse of ${P}_{C\\to B}$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{B\\to C} & ={P}_{C\\to B}^{-1} \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}7 & 3 \\\\ 9 & 4\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7\\cdot 4-3\\cdot 9}[\\begin{aligned}4 & -3 \\\\ -9 & 7\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =1\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}4 & -3 \\\\ -9 & 7\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & -3 \\\\ -9 & 7\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186247362 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186246829 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186247362 #edb/ref 1069 "q-113515" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186247362 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186247362 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $B={[\\begin{aligned}17 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}$  be a basis of ${R}^{2}$ and let $S$ be the standard basis of ${R}^{2}.$ Find the change-of-coordinates matrix from $S$ to $B.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186247362 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "cb921b26-a20c-5f23-a094-49ac99b2c6bd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186247362 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ and $S$ as ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ and ${{e}_{1},{e}_{2}}$, respectively. Recall that the standard basis of ${R}^{2}$ consists of the vectors\n\n$$\n{e}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{e}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $S$ to $B$ is a matrix whose columns are the coordinates of the vectors of $S$ relative to the basis $B.$\n\nHence, we need to find the coordinates of ${e}_{1}$ and ${e}_{2}$ relative to the basis $B.$ We can do this simultaneously by considering the matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | & | \\\\ {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} & {e}_{1} & {e}_{2} \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we simply reduce the left-hand side to reduced row-echelon form using Gaussian elimination: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}17 & 4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{17}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & \\frac{4}{17} & \\frac{1}{17} & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-4){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & \\frac{4}{17} & \\frac{1}{17} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{17} & -\\frac{4}{17} & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=17{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & \\frac{4}{17} & \\frac{1}{17} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -4 & 17\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-\\frac{4}{17}){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -4 & 17\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the right-hand side, we obtain the change-of-coordinates matrix from $S$ to $B.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n{P}_{S\\to B}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 17\\end{aligned}].\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394305 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186394306 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394305 #edb/ref 1069 "q-81859" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394305 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394305 #edb/ref 1071 "The change-of-coordinates matrix from the basis $C$ to the basis $B$ is given by\n${P}_{C\\to B}=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 2 \\\\ -9 & -3\\end{aligned}].$ Find the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C$  ." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394305 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a27a3c1e-ad3c-56e4-aa1a-d2aedbc7058d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394305 #edb/ref 1074 "The change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C$ is the inverse of the change-of-coordinates matrix from $C$ to $B.$ Therefore, we simply need to find the inverse of the given matrix.\n\nComputing the inverse of ${P}_{C\\to B}$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{B\\to C} & ={P}_{C\\to B}^{-1} \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}5 & 2 \\\\ -9 & -3\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5\\cdot (-3)-2\\cdot (-9)}[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -2 \\\\ 9 & 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-3 & -2 \\\\ 9 & 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ 3 & \\frac{5}{3}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394306 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186394307 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394306 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186394308 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394306 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186394309 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394306 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186394310 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394306 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186394311 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394306 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394306 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394306 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186394311 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394306 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "5d1bd593-f01f-5378-b1fe-6d41f9f01e52" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394307 #edb/ref 1080 "[\\begin{aligned}-1 & \\frac{2}{3} \\\\ -3 & \\frac{5}{3}\\end{aligned}]" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394307 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186394307 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "cb6b89d9-5ecc-523c-